        "math_academy_id": "q-103692",
        "problem": "If $sec\u2061x=\\sqrt{7},$ then what is $sec\u2061(x-16\\pi )?$",
        "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061x=sec\u2061(x+2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot \\pi )=sec\u2061(x-16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec\u2061x=\\sqrt{7},$ then $sec\u2061(x-16\\pi )=\\sqrt{7}.$",
